# Migrating BackgroundProcessor to .NET 8

## Key Breaking Change

`Thread.Abort()` and `Thread.ResetAbort()` throw `PlatformNotSupportedException` in .NET 8. The entire cancellation pattern must be replaced.

## Migrated Code

```csharp
public class BackgroundProcessor : IAsyncDisposable
{
    private Task? _workerTask;
    private CancellationTokenSource? _cts;

    public void Start()
    {
        _cts = new CancellationTokenSource();
        _workerTask = Task.Run(() => ProcessLoop(_cts.Token));
    }

    private async Task ProcessLoop(CancellationToken token)
    {
        while (!token.IsCancellationRequested)
        {
            try
            {
                // Pass token so blocking calls become cancellable
                var item = await GetNextItemAsync(token);
                await ProcessItemAsync(item, token);
            }
            catch (OperationCanceledException) when (token.IsCancellationRequested)
            {
                // Graceful shutdown requested
                RollbackCurrentTransaction();
                break;
            }
            finally
            {
                FlushBuffers();
            }
        }
    }

    public async ValueTask Stop(TimeSpan? timeout = null)
    {
        if (_cts is null || _workerTask is null)
            return;

        // Signal cooperative cancellation
        await _cts.CancelAsync(); // .NET 8+; avoids reentrancy issues vs .Cancel()

        // Wait with a hard timeout
        var effectiveTimeout = timeout ?? TimeSpan.FromSeconds(5);
        if (_workerTask != await Task.WhenAny(_workerTask, Task.Delay(effectiveTimeout)))
        {
            // Task didn't finish in time — log a warning.
            // There is NO forced-kill equivalent in .NET 8.
            Log.Warning("Worker did not exit within timeout.");
        }
    }

    public async ValueTask DisposeAsync()
    {
        await Stop();
        _cts?.Dispose();
    }
}
```

## Critical Pitfalls

| Pitfall | Explanation |
|---------|-------------|
| **No forced termination** | .NET 8 has no `Thread.Abort` replacement. If `GetNextItem()` blocks indefinitely without accepting a `CancellationToken`, your `Stop()` will hang. You *must* make blocking calls cancellable. |
| **`volatile bool` is insufficient** | A `volatile` flag can't interrupt a blocking I/O call. `CancellationToken` integrates with `async` APIs and `WaitHandle`s. |
| **`ThreadAbortException` never fires** | Any `catch (ThreadAbortException)` is dead code on .NET 8. Migrate cleanup logic into `OperationCanceledException` handling. |
| **`Thread.ResetAbort()` throws** | It's a `PlatformNotSupportedException` — remove all references. |
| **`CancellationTokenSource.Cancel()` reentrancy** | `.Cancel()` executes registered callbacks synchronously on the caller. Prefer `CancelAsync()` (.NET 8+) to avoid deadlocks. |
| **Unobserved task exceptions** | Unlike threads, unobserved `Task` exceptions don't crash the process by default — they're silently swallowed. Await the task or attach a continuation. |

## If You Can't Make Blocking Calls Async

For legacy sync code that truly can't accept a token (e.g., third-party library):

```csharp
// Last resort: dedicate a thread you can abandon (it leaks but won't throw)
_workerThread = new Thread(() =>
{
    while (!_cts.Token.IsCancellationRequested)
    {
        var item = GetNextItem(); // legacy blocking call
        if (_cts.Token.IsCancellationRequested) break;
        ProcessItem(item);
    }
});
_workerThread.IsBackground = true; // dies with process
_workerThread.Start();
```

Setting `IsBackground = true` ensures the thread won't keep the process alive on shutdown — but there is **no clean mid-operation abort**. Design your system so blocking calls have their own timeout or can be interrupted via closing an underlying resource (socket, stream, etc.).
